---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';
import PlusValueCalculator from '../../components/calc/PlusValueCalculator';
import MiniSim from '../../components/calc/MiniSim';
import ContentTable from '../../components/ContentTable.astro';
import { YEAR } from '../../data/site-config';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { $, pct, P } from '../../lib/tables';
import { abattRows, pvExemples, pvCalc } from '../../lib/immo-tables';
import { surtaxePlusValue, abattementsDetention } from '../../lib/engine/immobilier';
import { src } from '../../lib/sources';
const lang = 'en';
const V = P.plus_value;
const ex = pvCalc({ prixVente: 320000, prixAchat: 200000, annees: 12, depAchat: '33' });
const ex15 = abattementsDetention(15);
const gros = pvCalc({ prixVente: 900000, prixAchat: 400000, annees: 10, depAchat: '75' });
const neuf = pvCalc({ prixVente: 380000, prixAchat: 300000, annees: 7, depAchat: '06', typeAchat: 'neuf' });
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Do I pay French capital gains tax if I live in the UK?', a: `Yes. A gain on French property is taxed in France whatever your country of residence, at ${pct(V.taux_ir, lang, 0)} income tax plus social charges, after the same allowances for years of ownership as a resident. The French notary deducts the tax from the sale price on completion. The UK may tax the gain too, with credit for the French tax under the tax treaty.` },
  { q: 'Do non-residents need a tax representative when selling in France?', a: `Not in most cases. Residents of an EU or EEA country with a tax treaty with France are exempt, as is any seller when the price does not exceed ${$(V.representant_fiscal_prix_max, lang)} or when the gain is fully exempt through length of ownership. Others must appoint an accredited representative, which adds a fee of its own. The notary tells you early in the sale.` },
  { q: 'What if I move back to France, or leave it, before selling?', a: `A former main home stays exempt if you sell it by 31 December of the year after you moved your tax residence abroad and did not let or lend it in between. An EU or EEA national who lived in France for at least two years can also exempt up to ${$(V.non_resident_exoneration_plafond, lang)} of gain on one other home, within ten years of leaving.` },
  { q: 'Which social charges rate applies to my property gain?', a: `${pct(V.taux_ps, lang, 1)} for residents of France, the rate kept for property gains while other investment income moved to ${pct(P.location.ps_meuble, lang, 1)}. Non-residents affiliated to the compulsory social security system of another EEA country or of Switzerland pay only the ${pct(V.ps_affilie_eee_suisse, lang, 1)} solidarity levy, on a sworn statement, a point to raise with the notary before completion.` },
  { q: 'Are renovation costs on my French second home deductible?', a: `Yes, if they are construction, extension or improvement works, documented by French invoices from registered businesses, not routine maintenance or DIY materials. After ${V.forfait_travaux_detention_min_ans} years of ownership you may instead use a flat ${pct(V.forfait_travaux, lang, 0)} of the purchase price without any invoice; the calculator keeps whichever is higher.` },
  { q: 'Is the surtax on large gains charged to non-residents?', a: `Yes, on the same scale as residents: from 2% to 6% once the gain taxable to income tax, after allowances, exceeds ${$(V.surtaxe_seuil, lang)}, with smoothing at the start of each band. The official example in the tax administration’s doctrine puts it at ${$(surtaxePlusValue(103400), lang)} on a taxable gain of ${$(103400, lang)}.` },
];
const related = [
  { href: route('plusvalue', lang), name: 'Notary fees and capital gains', description: 'Actual costs or the 7.5% flat rate.' },
  { href: route('rendement', lang), name: 'Rental yield', description: 'Gross, net and net-net on a French rental.' },
  { href: route('home', lang), name: 'Notary fees calculator', description: 'What the purchase cost you, by département.' },
  { href: route('taxefonciere', lang), name: 'Property tax', description: 'The annual cost of keeping the home.' },
];
---
<GuideLayout lang={lang} id="plusvalueimmo" breadcrumbLabel="Capital gains on property"
  title={`Capital gains tax on French property ${YEAR}: calculator`}
  h1={`Capital gains tax on French property ${YEAR}: what the notary deducts on sale`}
  description={`Capital gains tax on French property ${YEAR}: ${pct(V.taux_ir, lang, 0)} tax plus social charges, allowances for years owned, surtax and exemptions. Second homes, non-residents.`}
  intro="Original notary fees recalculated for your département, flat rates, allowances and surtax: the calculation the seller’s notary will make."
  resume={`When you sell French property that is not your main home, a second home, a rental, a plot or a garage, the gain is taxed in France, even if you live abroad. The gain is the sale price minus a purchase price increased by acquisition costs (actual, or a flat ${pct(V.forfait_frais_acquisition, lang, 1)} of the price) and works (actual, or a flat ${pct(V.forfait_travaux, lang, 0)} after ${V.forfait_travaux_detention_min_ans} years). Two allowances for length of ownership then apply: the income tax one, at a ${pct(V.taux_ir, lang, 0)} rate, wipes the gain out after 22 years; the social charges one, at ${pct(V.taux_ps, lang, 1)} for French residents, after 30 years. A surtax of 2% to 6% applies above ${$(V.surtaxe_seuil, lang)} of taxable gain. A flat bought for ${$(200000, lang)} in the Gironde and sold for ${$(320000, lang)} twelve years later shows a gross gain of ${$(ex.pvBrute, lang)}; the notary deducts ${$(ex.total, lang)} from the price. The calculator recalculates your original notary fees to see whether they beat the flat rate.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={related}
  sources={src(lang, 'sp_plus_value', 'bofip_tpvie', 'sp_ps', 'sp_frais_notaire')}>
  <Fragment slot="tool"><div class="mb-8"><PlusValueCalculator client:load lang={lang} methodHref={route('method', lang)} /></div></Fragment>
  <MiniSim client:visible kind="pvduree" lang={lang} href={route('plusvalueimmo', lang)} />
  <h2>How French law measures the gain</h2>
  <p>France does not tax the difference between two prices: it taxes the difference between the sale price and an increased purchase price. The sale price is the one in the deed, less selling costs you can document, such as the compulsory surveys. The purchase price is raised by the acquisition costs you paid at the time, transfer tax, notary fees and an agency commission if you bore it, either at their actual amount or as a flat {pct(V.forfait_frais_acquisition, lang, 1)}. It is raised again by construction, extension or improvement works, on invoices, or by a flat {pct(V.forfait_travaux, lang, 0)} of the price once you have owned the property for more than {V.forfait_travaux_detention_min_ans} years.</p>
  <p>Many foreign owners no longer have the notary’s statement from their purchase. The calculator does not need it: it recalculates the notary fees of the original purchase with this site’s engine for the département you choose and keeps the actual figure if it beats the flat rate. On a resale purchase in a 5% département it usually does; on a new build it never does, because transfer tax is then reduced. For a flat bought new on the Riviera for {$(300000, lang)} and sold seven years later for {$(380000, lang)}, the flat rate wins and the total tax comes to {$(neuf.total, lang)}.</p>
  <h2>Allowances for years of ownership</h2>
  <p>The gross gain is then reduced according to full years of ownership, counted from the date of the purchase deed to the date of the sale deed. There are two scales. For income tax, the allowance is {pct(V.abattement_ir.taux, lang, 0)} a year from year 6 to year 21 and {pct(V.abattement_ir.annee22, lang, 0)} in year 22, so the gain is fully exempt after 22 years. For social charges, it is {pct(V.abattement_ps.taux, lang, 2)} a year from year 6 to year 21, {pct(V.abattement_ps.annee22, lang, 2)} in year 22 and {pct(V.abattement_ps.de23a30, lang, 0)} a year up to year 30. After fifteen years you keep paying income tax on {pct(100 - ex15.ir, lang, 0)} of the gain and social charges on {pct(100 - ex15.ps, lang, 2)}.</p>
  <ContentTable headers={['Ownership', 'Income tax allowance', 'Social charges allowance']} align={['l', 'r', 'r']} rows={abattRows(lang)} caption="French tax code, art. 150 VC; service-public.fr examples: 10 years → 30% and 8.25%; 25 years → 100% and 55%." />
  <h2>Rates, surtax and the amount withheld</h2>
  <p>The income tax base is taxed at {pct(V.taux_ir, lang, 0)} and the social charges base at {pct(V.taux_ps, lang, 1)} for a French resident. Above {$(V.surtaxe_seuil, lang)} of gain taxable to income tax, after the allowance, a surtax of 2% to 6% applies, smoothed at the start of each band so that one extra euro of gain never costs hundreds. A Paris flat bought for {$(400000, lang)} and sold for {$(900000, lang)} ten years later pays {$(gros.surtaxe, lang)} of surtax within a total of {$(gros.total, lang)}. The seller’s notary calculates all of it, files the return and pays the tax out of the sale proceeds on completion day.</p>
  <ContentTable headers={['Purchase', 'Sale', 'Ownership', 'Gross gain', 'Income tax', 'Social charges', 'Surtax', 'Total']} align={['r', 'r', 'l', 'r', 'r', 'r', 'r', 'r']} rows={pvExemples(lang)} caption="Resale purchases in Paris; actual acquisition costs recalculated by the engine; flat-rate works after 5 years; resident rates." />
  <h2>Selling from abroad</h2>
  <p>For a seller living outside France, the gain is calculated in exactly the same way. Two differences matter. The social charges: a seller affiliated to the compulsory social security system of another EEA country or of Switzerland pays only the {pct(V.ps_affilie_eee_suisse, lang, 1)} solidarity levy instead of the {pct(V.taux_ps, lang, 1)} charged to residents, according to service-public.fr, which lowers the bill noticeably; the calculator shows the resident rate, so ask the notary to apply your status. And the tax representative, whom the seller must appoint unless an exemption applies, notably for EU and EEA residents of treaty countries or a price of {$(V.representant_fiscal_prix_max, lang)} or less.</p>
  <p>The country where you live may tax the same gain under its own rules. Most treaties signed by France give it the first right to tax gains on French property and require the country of residence to give credit for the French tax. Keep the notary’s calculation and the receipt for the tax paid: you will need both for your home tax return.</p>
  <h2>Exemptions that remove the tax</h2>
  <p>Your main home is fully exempt, whatever the gain. A sale price of {$(V.exoneration_prix_max, lang)} or less is exempt too. The first sale of a home other than your main residence is exempt in proportion to the share of the price you reinvest in buying your main home within two years, if you did not own your main home in the four previous years. A former main home sold soon after leaving France, or up to {$(V.non_resident_exoneration_plafond, lang)} of gain on another home for an EU or EEA national who lived in France for two years, can also be exempt. Exemptions for modest retirees and for people moving into a care home depend on income ceilings and are checked by the notary on documents.</p>
  <h2>Limits of the calculation</h2>
  <p>The calculator assumes a private individual selling a property bought for a price. For an inherited or gifted property, enter the value declared in the deed as the purchase price and add the costs actually paid in the advanced options: the {pct(V.forfait_frais_acquisition, lang, 1)} flat rate does not apply. Depreciation claimed on a furnished let since 15 February 2025, shares in a property company and building plots follow their own rules. The figure is an estimate: the notary’s calculation is the one that counts.</p>
</GuideLayout>
